this is quite possibly the most  controversial graph in the history of  science and last month it got one hell  of an update  thanks to curiosity stream and nebula  for sponsoring this video this is the  so-called hockey stick a graph of the  past 1000 years of the earth's average  surface temperature created in 1999 by  scientists raymond bradley malcolm  hughes and michael mann now depending on  who you are you may be very familiar  with this figure as either putting  recent global warming in shocking  context or evidence of one of the most  diabolical scams in the history of  science but before getting to that let's  quickly talk about how it was made  obviously people weren't taking accurate  global average temperature readings back  in 1000 ad so to work out what the  temperature was back then the authors  used what's known as proxy measurements  imagine that you want to know how many  monkeys have lived in a particular  forest for the past century you know  that there are 1 000 monkeys this year  there were 980 last year and 1020 the  year before that but you don't know this  number any further into the past you do  however know how many mango seeds have  been found on the floor for the past 100  years scientists are weird and like  measuring things don't question it the  number of mango seeds on the floor will  correlate with the number of monkeys the  more monkeys there are the more mangoes  they eat and the more seeds they drop on  the forest floor so given the last three  years of data knowing both the number of  monkeys and the number of mango seeds  performing some statistical analysis you  can estimate the number of monkeys  present over the past 100 years we say  that the mango seeds act as a proxy  measurement for the number of monkeys  obviously it's not a perfect proxy  because the number of mango seeds found  on the forest floor could be affected by  other factors like the weather if it's  windy then mangoes will be blown off of  the trees and onto the forest floor or  disease or other animals that eat  mangoes so to improve our estimate of  the number of monkeys in the forest we  could incorporate multiple proxies into  our calculation so for example we could  look at the number of mango seeds found  on the forest floor every year but also  if there's a town nearby that gets  raided by the monkeys the number of  vegetables that are stolen from that  town's gardens every year the 1999  hockey stick effectively did this with  temperature in the northern hemisphere  estimating it using 12 proxy  measurements spread across the globe  those proxies were mostly tree ring data  so if you drill into the center of a  tree and extract a core you can see how  long the tree has been alive by counting  the alternating bands of growth that  correspond to the different seasons  but because trees grow more in warmer  years while the number of bands will  tell you how long the tree has been  alive the thickness of each year's band  will tell you how warm that year was as  in the monkey example however doing this  isn't a perfect estimate especially if  you're trying to estimate a global  temperature from just a handful of  proxies and the authors were very clear  in 1999 about this you can see how large  the uncertainty in the estimate is up  until about 400 years ago when  additional proxy data became available  that just tighten things up the  conclusion of this graph is pretty  shocking the global average temperature  was quite consistent up until the  industrial revolution at which point  humans started emitting lots of  greenhouse gases into the atmosphere and  the temperature  shot up so the graph looks like a hockey  stick it's got this long straight ish  handle and then there's a short sharp  upturn at the end which is like the  blade of the hockey stick and it was  interpreted as being pretty conclusive  evidence that humans were impacting the  global climate and causing it to warm  and the graph became associated in  particular with the ipcc's third  assessment report but was that  conclusion actually correct a flurry of  papers follow the 1999 hockey stick  claiming that the scientists and in  particular michael mann became kind of  the center of attention had bungled the  statistics a particular claim was that  based on a different analysis of the  same data the handle of the hockey stick  should have been a lot curvier in  particular there was the so-called  medieval warm period when temperatures  were higher than at present a few  centuries ago and then in the early  modern period a few centuries after that  there was a cooler time so observed 20th  century warming was actually just kind  of a readjustment out of that cooler  period and thus the humans weren't  warming the climate anywhere near as  much as man et al claimed this escalated  into a whole thing called climate gate  which involved a hack of emails and  multiple legal cases and lots of threats  being made against climate scientists  and mandy tells it all in his book the  hockey stick and the climate wars which  i may end up doing a video just about  this one day let me know in the comments  if you would like a video just about  climategate but the short version is  that in the past 20 years dozens of  independent studies have come out that  have pretty much verified the shape of  the hockey stick pretty flat with this  uptick at the end it turns out that the  medieval warm period just wasn't as warm  as some authors thought and that it was  actually more of a regional phenomenon  rather than a global warming as more  proxy data sets have become available  from tree rings but also things like  cave deposits and lake sediments and  oxygen isotope analysis and corals and  proxy data sets are kind of wild we've  become more confident in the hockey  stick and we shrunk the uncertainties  basically the conclusion of the original  man bradley and hughes paper has been  validated however last month a new paper  came out that did things a little  differently and made a big leap in how  we can reconstruct past temperatures  osmond et al published in nature created  what we call a re-analysis  normally when we talk about re-analyses  it's in the context of modern climate  data what they are is this combination  of actual observations and statistical  modeling to fill in the gaps where we  don't have weather stations or we don't  have accurate satellite data or balloon  data so it gives us this global  continuous data set that's really  valuable when you're doing research what  this paper did was construct the first  global reanalysis of past surface  temperature it basically plotted out  where hundreds of proxy measurements  were taken using a 200 year resolution  so we have an indirect measurement of  surface temperature at these locations  averaged over 200 years then the  researchers for the first time used  bayesian modeling techniques to fill in  the gaps between the proxies so they  estimated the spatial distribution of  surface temperature they recreated the  map of average surface temperature if  you like in 200-year chunks informed by  how we see temperatures change between  these proxy locations today this took  you may not be surprised to hear a lot  of work it took the scientists seven  years to produce this statistical model  drawing on disciplines like hydrology  and geochemistry and biology and  lots of specialist knowledge into this  one  frankly remarkable statistical model the  result is pretty amazing the hockey  stick now goes back 24 000 years we can  really clearly see the earth coming out  of the last ice age and how since then  for the past 5000 years or more the  earth's global temperature has been very  consistent the handle of the hockey  stick is longer than before and  straighter than before that puts modern  warming in even sharper context because  previous studies that had basically just  taken averages of the proxy measurements  had found that the earth had been  cooling over the past 10 000 years or so  and thus  at some point in the relatively recent  past the earth had actually been as warm  as today if not a little warmer whereas  this far more sophisticated analysis  found the opposite they found that the  earth had been very slightly warming and  thus the modern human-induced climate  change caused by the emission of  greenhouse gases was pushing the earth's  climate to a place that it hadn't been  for  a very long time at least since before  the last ice age and yes that's another  aspect of this paper the authors  disentangled the likely causes of the  observed cooling and warming over the 24  000 year time period considered about  three and a half percent of the warming  is due to changes in the earth's orbit  and ocean circulations with the rest  being due to changes in greenhouse gases  and surface reflectivity as when the ice  age ended the earth became less  reflective note that that's not just  human emissions of greenhouse gases it  actually refers to any changes in the  concentration of greenhouse gases in the  atmosphere human on short time scales  and natural on much longer time scales  this is an amazing paper it puts the  modern warming that we've seen over the  past couple of hundred years into even  starker contrast because it shows that  it's just unprecedented in the recent  record and further that by emitting more  and more greenhouse gases into the  atmosphere and pushing the global  average temperature up and up  every year we are pushing the world's  climate further and further from  anything that we as modern humans have  ever experienced  before the science has come a long way  since the original hockey stick paper  and we understand the mechanism by which  this warming is taking place and its  historical context better than ever  before we understand it really very well  now  so can we please do something about this  changes in global and regional climate  have re-written human history and quite  possibly led to homo sapiens rather than  neanderthals being the dominant species  on this planet  this is covered in fascinating detail in  how climate made history one of the  thousands of documentaries available on  curiosity stream this video is sponsored  curiosity stream is the number one  source of professional productions on  topics from climate to politics history  to  samurai swords  think netflix but smart instead of  covering you with crisps and regret  watching it actually makes you better  informed about the world if however  you'd prefer something a bit more indie  something with that bit more of a  homespun personal touch then look no  further than nebula the streamy award  nominated service owned and operated by  a bunch of youtubers like me who wanted  independence from well  youtube we don't like ads so nebula has  none of them in the videos or on its  pages instead it's a subscription-based  service with your money funding  exclusive original content like  technorama about how movies portray  technology as well as directly  supporting creators on the platform like  me also we are finally now available on  your tv via our roku app but why have i  told you about these two services  because you can get both of them in a  bundle signing up at curiositystream.com  simon clark you can get access to  curiositystream and nebula and get a 26  discount on both just 14.79 a year for  the best documentaries on the internet  and finance exciting new educational  content that's curiositystream.com  simon clark to improve your viewing  experience online and support this  channel  thank you very much for watching this  video this was an installment in my  series going through recent papers and  atmospheric science and kind of  translating them for a popular audience  and i love making these videos so i  really hope that you liked it if you did  please do pop it a like if you do want  to check out some previous episodes in  this series then here